<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
         │ NO-NETWORK INVARIANT — do not add a networking entitlement here.     │
         └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
         SpectiX makes a public promise that it performs no network activity
         (README "Privacy: no network", spectix.app/privacy).
         Users are told to audit that promise by dumping this very list back out
         of the shipped binary with codesign (the exact command is in README.md
         "Privacy" and docs/permissions.md — it cannot be repeated here, see the
         XML note at the bottom), so anything added below becomes a public claim
         the moment it ships.
         Never add com.apple.security.network.client / .network.server, and
         never add an App Sandbox key to "make the promise stronger" — the app
         reads arbitrary paths under ~/.claude and drives other apps through
         AXUIElement, neither of which survives the sandbox. The guarantee is
         upheld by there being no networking code at all (build.sh enforces
         this at compile time), not by a sandbox denying it at runtime.

         The hardened runtime (required for notarization) blocks Apple Events by
         default. Jump-to-terminal spawns /usr/bin/osascript to raise Terminal /
         VSCode windows (see spawnResponsible in main.swift), so without this the
         whole jump path silently dies on notarized builds.
         Accessibility (AXUIElement) and reading ~/.claude need no entitlement —
         they are TCC grants, not runtime capabilities.

         XML note: a comment may never contain two consecutive hyphens, so no
         command line carrying a long option can be quoted in here at all.
         Point at README.md / docs/permissions.md instead. Getting this wrong
         breaks the file only on the release path — build.sh passes this file to
         codesign solely when SIGN_ID is set, so an ad-hoc local build stays
         green and the damage only surfaces mid-notarization. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.automation.apple-events</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
